# Explicit preferred funder group names

## Problem

The component identity is the minimum surviving UUID, whereas its display name is currently that identity's recorded name. These are separate concerns. A BBC component may have an unrelated alias-connected representative; neither changing that representative nor rewriting its source name is acceptable. Reviewed labels belong to the grouping projection, with policy ownership remaining in `domain/models/funder_grouping.rs` and `funder_families.v1.json`.

## Usage (caller's view)

```rust
// Existing ingestion and refresh call sites remain unchanged.
let groups = build_funder_groups(&funders)?;
// outbound projects memberships, evidence, and groups.preferred_names in one transaction.

db.refresh_funder_groups().await?;
let matches = db.get_funders_by_text_search_score(&request("BBC", 10, 1.0)).await?;
// Same minimum UUID; display "BBC", including when no recorded funder is exactly BBC.
let details = db.funder_funding(member_id).await?;
// Existing requested-ID contract stays; profile.name is BBC; recorded aliases/evidence unchanged.
```

## Shape

Use a sparse, pipeline-owned `exposed.funder_group_names` table with one row per component having an unambiguous reviewed preferred name. Ordinary components continue using their representative's current recorded name. This avoids materializing a redundant fallback name for every group. The domain builder remains the sole policy owner; SQL readers only select persisted labels.

Types inside `domain/models/funder_grouping.rs`:

```rust
#[derive(Clone, Debug, Deserialize)]
#[serde(try_from = "String")]
struct PreferredFunderName(String);
impl TryFrom<String> for PreferredFunderName {
    type Error = &'static str;
    fn try_from(value: String) -> Result<Self, Self::Error> {
        // Reject whitespace-only; trim exterior whitespace, preserve interior spelling/case.
        todo!()
    }
}
impl PreferredFunderName {
    fn as_str(&self) -> &str { todo!() }
}
struct ReviewedFamily {
    key: String,
    preferred_name: PreferredFunderName,
    names: Vec<String>,
}
pub(crate) struct FunderGroupName {
    pub(crate) group_id: FunderId,
    pub(crate) preferred_name: PreferredFunderName,
    pub(crate) family_key: String,
}
pub struct FunderGroups {
    // Existing memberships and evidence unchanged.
    pub(crate) preferred_names: Vec<FunderGroupName>,
}
// Add domain error:
// ConflictingReviewedFamilies { group_id: FunderId, family_keys: Vec<String> }
```

Adjust visibility for `PreferredFunderName` and `as_str` to `pub(crate)` because the outbound projection consumes the typed name; construction stays private/parsed. No accessor on policy or runtime policy lookup is exposed. Labels in JSON are explicitly BBC, GB News, Premier League. Names used for matching remain exactly the existing reviewed names; preferred labels are not grouping evidence.

Algorithm: retain existing alias and reviewed-family joins. During the family pass, record `(member_id, family_index)` for every canonical-name match, including a single matching member; do not derive label assignments from pairwise links. After *all* joins, map these memberships through final roots into `BTreeMap<Uuid, BTreeSet<family_index>>`. Zero reviewed families => no metadata; one => its typed preferred name; more than one => a deterministic domain error containing root and sorted family keys. Reject ambiguity even if labels happen to coincide: equal labels do not establish a reviewed identity relationship. Build finishes before projection deletes, so conflict preserves the preceding valid snapshot and exposes bad policy/data rather than silently inventing precedence.

The public builder still hides union-find, policy parsing, conflict resolution, singleton treatment, and name ownership behind one call. This is boundary-discipline and foundational-thinking without adding services or runtime policy readers.

New reversible migration, never edit existing migrations:

```sql
CREATE TABLE exposed.funder_group_names (
    group_id uuid PRIMARY KEY REFERENCES exposed.funders(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    preferred_name text NOT NULL CHECK (btrim(preferred_name) <> ''),
    family_key text NOT NULL CHECK (btrim(family_key) <> '')
);
CREATE INDEX funder_group_names_preferred_name_trgm
    ON exposed.funder_group_names USING gin (preferred_name gin_trgm_ops);
-- down: DROP TABLE exposed.funder_group_names;
```

Use the repository's schema-qualified extension operator-class convention if required. No SQL policy backfill: the existing `refresh-funder-groups` operation projects the compiled policy after migration. Before refresh, left joins preserve prior behavior. Projection performs `DELETE FROM exposed.funder_group_names`, then inserts typed rows with `sqlx::query!` alongside current membership/evidence replacement under the existing loader lock and transaction. Repeated refresh converges; any SQL or domain failure rolls back all projections.

Text search: retain recorded-name candidate/index/scoring logic. Add preferred-name candidates as an independent `UNION ALL` branch into `qualified`, rather than merely adding representative IDs to existing candidates (that would score their recorded names instead of the searched label):

```sql
SELECT n.group_id AS id, n.preferred_name AS name,
       f.funder_kind AS kind, NULL::text AS matched_alias,
       word_similarity($1, n.preferred_name) AS similarity_score
FROM exposed.funder_group_names n
JOIN exposed.funders f ON f.id = n.group_id
WHERE n.preferred_name %> $1
  AND word_similarity($1, n.preferred_name) >= $2
```

`grouped` joins `funder_group_names n ON n.group_id = COALESCE(g.group_id,q.id)` and displays `COALESCE(n.preferred_name, representative.funder_name)`. Provenance is chosen from the winning candidate, not UUID inequality:

```sql
CASE
  WHEN q.matched_alias IS NOT NULL THEN q.matched_alias
  WHEN q.name <> COALESCE(n.preferred_name, representative.funder_name) THEN q.name
END AS matched_alias
```

This yields Name for a preferred-name hit and Alias with the original source spelling when a representative's recorded name wins but its display has changed. Preserve existing canonical-vs-alias tie semantics within each recorded candidate; add a deterministic tie breaker preferring the visible-name candidate when synthetic preferred and recorded rows share ID and score. Keep deduplication before limit. `has_individual` continues deriving from qualified kind.

Phonetic search: preserve individual-only behavior. Join preferred metadata for display and determine provenance using the final displayed name, just as text search. For completeness add a preferred-name branch to `names` filtered to representative kind Individual and the same name-code predicate; this does not make BBC/GB News organization names phonetic candidates. Existing `alias` stays explicit for recorded aliases; compare canonical candidate against selected display when deriving final provenance. The final projection must retain candidate text through winners to make this comparison possible.

Details: in the existing representative query, left join metadata on `f.id` and select `COALESCE(n.preferred_name,f.funder_name) AS "funder_name!"`. Preserve the requested ID in `Funder::new(id,...)`; aliases, evidence, aggregates, membership and recorded declaration rows are untouched. No preferred label is inserted into `funder_aliases` or the details recorded-alias list. Member history keeps source names.

## Synthesis decision

Candidate proposes the sparse group metadata table as the base. Orchestrator fills final synthesis.

## Tradeoffs accepted

- We accept one small table and joins in exchange for pipeline-owned names shared consistently by every reader.
- We accept rejecting components containing multiple reviewed families in exchange for explicit, reviewable ambiguity handling and no arbitrary naming precedence.
- We accept explicit refresh after migration in exchange for keeping the JSON policy the sole policy authority and avoiding SQL copies of family names.
- We accept a text candidate union in exchange for preferred labels being searchable without fabricated recorded aliases.

## Alternatives considered

- **Name columns on membership rows:** one existing table, but duplicates a component fact across every member. Partial writes can disagree, readers must know which row owns the label, and singleton and representative special cases leak into queries. A column only populated on the representative avoids duplication but creates a concealed second row role and still requires a second membership join.
- **Runtime policy lookup:** saves migration work but requires API and ingestion to load policy consistently; search must separately enumerate policy labels before ranking and pagination. Persisted membership and currently compiled policy can disagree. Too much policy escapes the builder.
- **Metadata row for every component:** internally tidy, but materializes ordinary representative names and introduces stale copies. Sparse reviewed-name metadata satisfies this bounded task with fewer invariants.

## Implementation reconciliation

No implementation yet. Existing details tests explicitly require requested funder ID, so preferred names must not accidentally canonicalize the detail response ID.

## Tests and risks

1. Domain tests: missing/empty/whitespace preferred name rejected; exact source names preserved; reviewed singleton receives label with no pairwise evidence; smaller unrelated alias-connected representative gets correct family label; order permutations produce same metadata; two reviewed families bridged by an alias fail deterministically even with equal labels.
2. SQL integration: only BBC Studios Ltd exists, exact BBC at threshold 1 yields one BBC result; minimum-ID alias bridge displays BBC; searching representative source yields Alias provenance; searching preferred label yields Name; any member details show BBC while preserving requested ID and recorded aliases.
3. Refresh twice gives identical metadata/membership/evidence; removing a family member removes stale label; ambiguity on a later refresh leaves previous database projection unchanged. Source-row snapshots establish no name/alias mutations.
4. Search regression: alias ranking, grouping before limit, individual eligibility, phonetic provenance, and ordinary fallback are preserved.
5. Isolated migration up with existing rows, down, up, refresh; compile sqlx macros against migrated database and refresh offline query data if repository requires it.
6. Real HTTP verification: BBC is one result with established 12 members and 71 entries; GB News/Premier League names and detail identity/amounts remain correct; member histories retain source names.

Should observed existing data already bridge multiple reviewed families, the conflict is a policy decision to resolve explicitly rather than silently weakening the ambiguity contract. No speculative API enum or provenance field is needed: existing Name/Alias source distinguishes preferred hits from recorded alternative spellings.

## Next implementation step

Add typed preferred-name policy parsing and final-component metadata construction, with singleton/bridge/ambiguity tests, before creating the migration and wiring the atomic projection.
